  "query_preview": "/Tuesday through Saturday/...\nA complex upper level pattern will continue to bring several \nrounds of precipitation, breezy winds, and fluctuating \ntemperatures through the long term period. Another closed low in\nthe Eastern Pacific will shift east on Tuesday, pushing a \npronounced atmospheric river into northern California, Oregon \nand southern Washington. Remnants of this substantial moisture \nwill move into the region on Wednesday, with some model \ndisagreement on how much moisture will make it into our region \non Wednesday. The best chances of precipitation will be over \nsoutheast Oregon on Wednesday, with high terrain in southwestern\nIdaho also seeing elevated chances.\n\nHigh pressure will build in on Thursday with a warm front \nbringing another push of precipitation and much higher snow \nlevels for Thursday. Some deterministic solutions show snow \nlevels reaching near 7000 feet MSL on Thursday, with current \nforecast favoring lower levels near 5500-6500 feet MSL. Dry and \nwarm conditions will continue through Saturday, with another \nsystem threatening to move into the region on Sunday. Model \nuncertainty remains high for next weekend's system, with current\nforecast favoring only a 20-30% chance of measurable \nprecipitation over the region.",
